<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zhizhi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/36-bce">36 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zhizhi was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanyu">chanyu</a> of the first century BCE, a brother and rival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huhanye">Huhanye</a> during the civil war that split the Xiongnu Empire. While Huhanye submitted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han Dynasty</a>, Zhizhi led his followers westward. Accounts of the Xiongnu Empire treat this division as a stage in the empire's decline; Zhizhi was later killed by a Han expedition in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zhizhi.jpg" alt="Zhizhi" width="320" height="252" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Postmann Michael · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hsiung-nu-Empire.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Zhizhi's original name in Chinese transcription was Luandi Hutuwusi; he was of the Luandi clan and one of the Worthy Princes of the East. When his father, Xulüquanqu Chanyu, died in 60 BCE, power was seized by a distant relative, Woyanqudi. In 58 BCE Huhanye, a younger son of Xulüquanqu, revolted and made himself chanyu, and Woyanqudi took his own life soon after.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 56 BCE Hutuwusi rose against his younger brother, took the title Zhizhi Chanyu and drove <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huhanye">Huhanye</a> out of the royal domain. Huhanye moved south, submitted to the Han in 53 BCE and used Chinese support against him. Zhizhi too offered tribute and sent a son as hostage, but the Han court favoured Huhanye. Growing weaker, Zhizhi began moving west in 49 BCE; he killed Yilimu, another claimant, fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wusun">Wusun</a>, gained control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dingling">Dingling</a> and made his capital among the Jiankun, who may have been the Yenisei Kirghiz.</p><p>By 44 BCE he was on the north slope of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian-shan">Tian Shan</a>. The Han returned his son, but for unknown reasons Zhizhi murdered the envoy who came with him. He made a marriage alliance with the rulers of Kangju near Lake Balkhash and led his tribe west; they suffered greatly from cold, and only 3,000 people arrived. With the Kangju he plundered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wusun">Wusun</a>, then quarrelled with his hosts, killed several hundred of them, forced them to build him a fortress on the Dulaishui River and took tribute from Dayuan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Fearing that Zhizhi meant to build a large empire, the Han commander of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-regions">Western Regions</a> struck first. In 36 BCE the generals Chen Tang and Gan Yanshou led 40,000 men through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wusun">Wusun</a> territory to Kangju, near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taraz">Taraz</a>. They beat off Kangju raiders and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> attack on their camp, then advanced on the city with shields and spears in front and crossbowmen behind, drained the moat and piled firewood against the palisade. The city fell, and Zhizhi was killed and beheaded; his head was presented to Emperor Yuan of Han at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>.</p><p>During the battle an infantry unit on the Kangju side fought in a formation said to look like fish scales, which has led to speculation that its men were Greek hoplites from Dayuan; the evidence is inconclusive.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zhizhi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the civil war of 60–53 BCE the chanyu Huhanye moved south and submitted to the Han, using Chinese support against his brother <b>Zhizhi</b>; the Han reduced the Xiongnu from a &quot;brotherly state&quot; to an &quot;outer vassal&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/empire/southern-xiongnu">Southern Xiongnu · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 51 BCE the chanyu Huhanye travelled in person to the Han court at Chang'an and acknowledged Han overlordship in return for protection and gifts, while his brother and rival <b>Zhizhi</b> withdrew westward, where he was killed by a Han expedition in 36 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 36 BCE it sheltered the Xiongnu chanyu <b>Zhizhi</b>, whom a Han army killed there.</blockquote><a href="/state/kangju">Kangju</a></li></ul></section>
